plex than  others for example a glock like this  would require a metal slider  among other additional metal elements  well i just don't want to give the  impression  that it's as easy as printing off a page  of a4 paper that's not the case you need  all of the correct components you need  the right designs you need to assemble  it correctly you need to test it with  ammunition which in the uk is not so  easy to come by and you need to prove  that it works and that it's reliable  since 2019 six people in the uk have  been prosecuted where 3d printed guns  played a role in their case  of them  five were linked to the far right i  wouldn't say the greatest risk comes  from the 3d printed gun movement on mass  radicalizing and carrying out  ideologically or politically motivated  violence i would say the greater risk  comes from  existing extremists  coming across these communities and due  to their lack security they can easily  join them download the right designs  and use them as they wish  in germany a neo-nazi killed two people  and attacked a synagogue in 2019  he wanted to show the power of 3d  printed guns and other improvised  weapons  the key point is will it inspire other  people to follow that same  attack method as we've seen with other  attack methods like vehicle rammings  stabbings and so on  stefan balliet in germany thought he  would be that person to inspire  a wave of copycat attacks but it wasn't  the case so in the future we may see  something similar like that by someone  who's dedicated to  proving the viability of a 3d printed  gun  so  do those who put these files online feel  any responsibility for where they end up  our only interest  is the freedom of human beings to  protect themselves  and frankly in our groups we have rules  where hateful  content like that hateful speech  disparagement of others especially based  on race is not tolerated currently 3d  printed guns make up only a fraction of  the met police's work with the force  seizing 